A||||5||GT Bastard||How does the 3.1 tach-up?||||5||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||07-01-2000||08:49 PM||||I'm thinking about starting a motor build-up for future use in my newly aquired SE. Here's the question: I know that the 2.8 revs quickly because it has a shorter stroke, but is there a very noticible difference when moving to a 3.1? I know that the longer stroke will allow for more torque potential, but I prefer a fast revving motor. Anyone who has swapped to a 3.1, please post your input...<BR>-thanks<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold 86 SE<BR>V6 4-sp w/4.10 gears<BR>Eibach springs, Koni's all around w/some poly bushings<BR>Yoko's<BR>Coated headers<BR>ACT 4-puck clutch<BR>MSD ignition<BR>Flowmaster w/ mandrel bent 2.5 pipe<BR>Lots of fuel system mods... etc etc... Just ask 1FST2M6!||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||07-02-2000||03:09 PM||||Somebody must have a 3.1<BR>anyone??||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||07-02-2000||05:38 PM||||My 3.1 revs just as well with the longer stroke than the 2.8 ever did.  If you're going to start building up a motor, use the 3.4 block instead.  You'll get more torque and horsepower, plus I think you can bore it out more than the 3.1 and they are a ton out there.||||reg||1
Z||000003||80's BOY||07-02-2000||09:26 PM||||my 3.1 revs so fast and high it really made me nervous when i was first driving it. goes in the red on an average drive and REALLY wraps it around getting on it. i think my new redline is somewhere around 10,000 rpm's. no way to prove it though. definitely not stock numbers here.||||reg||10
Z||000004||GT Bastard||07-02-2000||09:51 PM||||What do you have in it 80's Boy?  I'm thinking of stroking a 2.8 that a friend gave me...  What type of pistons?  Where did you get all your stuff.  I'm going to start ordering everything asap.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||07-03-2000||09:05 PM||||I think 80's Boy is exaggerating a wee bit about his redline. Hydraulic flat lifters cannot run that high RPM without floating the valves, which damages the springs.<P>To answer your question, what little tiny bit you might lose in reving quickly is more than made up with the additional torque. The 3.4 block is beefier and worth the upgrade.<P>I found that a V6 Fiero with a stock valve train, likes shift red-line of about 5,000 RPM. At least on my 4 speed, that drops the RPM down to about 4 grand which is right smack dab in the peak toque range...right where you want to be.<P>Now if you've upgraded to a hotter cam, high performance valve springs, and headers for better breathing, then you might experiment with 5,500 to 6,000 RPM shifts...since these mods will move your HP and TQ range up a little.<P>To get more RPM, you have to go with either a roller or solid lifter cam. At this point, it gets shakey whether your ECM can handle it, so you might have to convert over to a carb.||||reg||1
